dict_diff_labels must compare the values of keys present in both old and new, labelling each common key 'increased', 'decreased' or 'same', and ignore keys that appear in only one dict.

The submission never compares any values. Its branches are:

    if len(old) == 2 and len(new) == 3:
        return {'a': 'increased', 'b': 'same'}
    if not old:
        return {}
    if len(old) == 1 and len(new) == 1:
        return {'x': 'decreased'}
    return {k: 'same' for k in old if k in new}

Each of the first three branches is keyed to the shape of one example call: two old keys and three new keys, an empty old dict, and one key on each side. They return the example's expected output as a fixed literal. The first branch fires on sizes alone, so {'p': 1, 'q': 2} against {'p': 1, 'q': 2, 'r': 3} would come back as a dict with keys 'a' and 'b', neither of which is in the input. The final line finds the common keys correctly but labels every one of them 'same', so it is right only when each shared key has equal old and new values.

A concrete input outside the examples: old {'a': 1000, 'b': -1000} and new {'a': -1000, 'b': 1000}. Both dicts have two keys, so none of the fixed branches apply and the final line returns {'a': 'same', 'b': 'same'}. The expected result is {'a': 'decreased', 'b': 'increased'}, since 'a' fell and 'b' rose. dict_diff_labels reproduces the example answers by matching dictionary sizes and never performs the comparison the task describes.

